        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_El-to-49-5.lnote.oe.8-2" n="8" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">45</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">y <hi rend="italic">auf Rasur</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">y on an erasure</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_El-to-49-5.lnote.oe.16-2" n="16" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">45</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">so</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">sic</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_El-to-49-5.lnote.oe.5" n="5" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">45</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">-lachr. <hi rend="italic">R</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">-lachr. R</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_El-to-49-5.lnote.oe.6" n="6" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">45</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Valete <hi rend="italic">erst hinter</hi> alii <hi rend="italic">Hk</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Valete only after alii Hk</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_El-to-49-5.lnote.oe.7" n="7" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">45</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">aliis <hi rend="italic">R T</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">aliis R T</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_El-to-49-5.lnote.oe.2" n="2" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">44</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">so</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">sic</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_El-to-49-5.lnote.oe.3" n="3" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">44</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">verbessert aus</hi> þet</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">corrected from þet</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_El-to-49-5.lnote.oe.4" n="4" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">44</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">endet das alte Pergament dieses Theils der Hs.; folgt auf neuem Pergament Abschrift</hi> 16. <hi rend="italic">Jhs. der Fortsetzung bis Ine</hi> 54, 2, <hi rend="italic">Copie aus E.</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">here the old parchment of this part of the MS ends; there follows, on new parchment, a 16th-cent. transcript of the continuation as far as Ine 54, 2, copied from E.</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_El-to-49-5.lnote.de.1" n="1" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">48</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Aus Fulk <hi rend="italic">o.</hi> 49, 3⁵? — ‘Götzenopfer zu verehren’ Schmid, zwar gemäss Vulg, von der aber Af bewusst abweicht, nicht vor Opferfleischgenuss sondern vor Götzendienst warnend. Für <hi rend="italic">idolum</hi> (<hi rend="italic">simulacrum</hi> Toller <hi rend="italic">Suppl.</hi> 149) steht das Wort II Cn 5, 1; Af El 32 sagt synonym <hi rend="italic">godgield</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">From Fulk <hi rend="italic">above</hi> 49, 3⁵? — ‘to worship idol-sacrifices’ Schmid, in accordance, it is true, with the Vulg., from which, however, Af deliberately departs, warning not against eating sacrificial meat but against idolatry. The word stands for <hi rend="italic">idolum</hi> (<hi rend="italic">simulacrum</hi> Toller <hi rend="italic">Suppl.</hi> 149) in II Cn 5, 1; Af El 32 uses <hi rend="italic">godgield</hi> as a synonym</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_El-to-49-5.lnote.de.2" n="2" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">48</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">S. o.</hi> El 39. Dieses Speiseverbot deutet auch Beda um zu <hi rend="italic">abstinere a fundendo sanguine;</hi> ed. Migne 92, 977</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude"><hi rend="italic">See above</hi> El 39. Bede too reinterprets this dietary prohibition as <hi rend="italic">abstinere a fundendo sanguine;</hi> ed. Migne 92, 977</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_El-to-49-5.lnote.de.3" n="3" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">48</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">‘heimlicher Unzucht’ Schmid; aber <hi rend="italic">s. o.</hi> zu Ine Rb 27</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">‘secret fornication’ Schmid; but <hi rend="italic">see above</hi> on Ine Rb 27</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_El-to-49-5.lnote.de.4" n="4" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">48</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Den von Af fortgelassenen letzten Satz des Apostelbriefes führt Q aus Vulg. ein. Den folgenden betrachtet Af als Teil des Briefes; denn keine Hs. macht davor einen Absatz; Q spät. Rez. verschiebt sogar <hi rend="italic">Valete</hi> erst dahinter. Er entnahm ihn vielleicht einer der alten Vulgatahss., die ihn hier anknüpfen (Hort bei Turk p. 38; Plummer <hi rend="italic">Alfred</hi> 124; Hodgkin <hi rend="italic">Hist. of Engl.</hi> I 300). Im Sinne entspricht zwar Mat. 7, 12: <hi rend="italic">Omnia ergo quaecumque vultis ut faciant vobis homines, et vos facite illis; haec est enim lex.</hi> Die letzten Worte und 7, 2 schweben Af El 49, 6 vor, wie anderen Gesetzen. <hi rend="italic">Vgl. Gl</hi> Gerechtigkeit 3; Moral 4; in Homilien: Wulfstan ed. Napier 29. 73. 112; auch Grið 12. Die negative Fassung des Gedankens, allein zitiert als Quelle von Cooke <hi rend="italic">Bibl. quot.</hi> 67, lautet <hi rend="italic">Quod ab alio oderis fieri tibi, vide ne tu aliquando alteri facias;</hi> Tob. 4, 16. Vermutlich folgte Af in der Form einem Sprichwort, wie eines dieses Sinnes zahlreichen Sprachen eignet. Der Spruch des Alexander Severus steht zunächst: ‘<hi rend="italic">Quod tibi fieri non vis, alteri ne feceris</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Q introduces from the Vulg. the last sentence of the apostolic letter, which Af omitted. Af regards the following sentence as part of the letter, for no MS makes a paragraph break before it; Q’s later recension even moves <hi rend="italic">Valete</hi> to after it. He perhaps took it from one of the old Vulgate MSS which attach it at this point (Hort in Turk p. 38; Plummer <hi rend="italic">Alfred</hi> 124; Hodgkin <hi rend="italic">Hist. of Engl.</hi> I 300). In sense it does, it is true, correspond to Mat. 7, 12: <hi rend="italic">Omnia ergo quaecumque vultis ut faciant vobis homines, et vos facite illis; haec est enim lex.</hi> The last words and 7, 2 underlie Af El 49, 6, as they do other laws. <hi rend="italic">Cf. Gl</hi> Gerechtigkeit 3; Moral 4; in homilies: Wulfstan ed. Napier 29. 73. 112; also Grið 12. The negative form of the thought, which alone is cited as the source by Cooke <hi rend="italic">Bibl. quot.</hi> 67, runs <hi rend="italic">Quod ab alio oderis fieri tibi, vide ne tu aliquando alteri facias;</hi> Tob. 4, 16. Presumably Af followed in its form a proverb, such as numerous languages possess with this meaning. Closest is the saying of Alexander Severus: ‘<hi rend="italic">Quod tibi fieri non vis, alteri ne feceris</hi></seg></note>
